<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aztec Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aztecs, Triple Alliance, Ēxcān Tlahtolōyān, Aztec</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aztec Empire was the Triple Alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a>, formed in 1428 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> and dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> of Tenochtitlan. It collected tribute from a large number of subject <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> across central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It was conquered by Spanish forces led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> with indigenous allies in 1519–1521, a foundational event of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aztec-empire.jpg" alt="Aztec Empire" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Keepscases; derivative work by Giggette · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aztec_Empire_ME_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> peoples descended from Chichimec peoples who migrated into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from the north in the early 13th century. Pictographic codices say their place of origin was Aztlán. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> as outsiders with limited resources, and military service was one of the few ways to win protection. They fought as subordinate allies of stronger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a>, paying tribute to its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a>. Acamapichtli, son of a Mexica leader and a daughter of a king of Culhuacan, was enthroned as the first tlatoani of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> in 1372.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a> died in 1426, a succession crisis became a civil war. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxtla">Maxtla</a> usurped the throne and turned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>, whose ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimalpopoca">Chimalpopoca</a> died soon after, possibly assassinated. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a>, whose king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a> had fled into exile, the dissident <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepanecs">Tepanec</a> city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a> and Huexotzinco in war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a>, and won in 1428. The three cities formed the Triple Alliance.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a> carried out sweeping reforms of state and religion. It has been alleged that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacaelel">Tlacaelel</a> ordered the burning of earlier books and rewrote Aztec history to give the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> a more central part. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a> replaced uncooperative kings with loyal rulers and set up tribute collectors who taxed people directly. Expansion reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, and was briefly halted by a four-year drought that began in 1450. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl">Axayacatl</a> spent most of a thirteen-year reign consolidating, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a>, from 1486, put down rebellions and conquered the Oaxaca Valley and the Soconusco coast. He took the title huehuetlatoani to set himself above the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> and Tlacopan.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> landed in Yucatán in 1519 with about 630 men. He gained two interpreters, Gerónimo de Aguilar and the Nahua-Maya woman known as La Malinche, who became a key figure in his dealings with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> rulers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The empire ruled indirectly. Local rulers were generally left in place so long as they paid tribute and sent troops, and in return received protection and a share in an integrated economy. At its height the state had 38 tributary provinces, supervised by high stewards called huecalpixque, with a separate official, the petlacalcatl, coordinating tribute at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. A restive conquered city might receive a military governor, a cuauhtlatoani. Provinces were either tributary, with mandatory obligations, or strategic, client states acting by mutual consent. The empire was not continuous territorially: Xoconochco lay apart from the centre.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> belief centred on teotl, a sacred power manifested in the supreme god Ometeotl and a large pantheon. The imperial cult was that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> war god Huītzilōpōchtli, whom conquered peoples had to add to their own gods, though they kept their traditions otherwise.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aztec Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the same moment Hernán Cortés began the conquest of the <b>Aztec Empire</b> (1519–1521), and Francisco Pizarro later toppled the Inca Empire (1532–1533).</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>New Spain was born from the conquest of the <b>Aztec Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From about 1460 this expansion met that of the Aztec <b>Triple Alliance</b>, and the two powers fought along a long frontier until the Spanish conquest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its original name is unknown; Teotihuacan is the Nahuatl name later given to it by the <b>Aztecs</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/teotihuacan">Teotihuacan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its leaders told him that the <b>Aztecs</b> had surrounded them for generations, so that they ate no salt and wore no cotton, which their cold country could not grow.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Nahuatl the word toltecatl, ‘Toltec’, came to mean a skilled artisan, and the <b>Aztecs</b> called art and refinement toltecayotl, ‘Toltec-ness’.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
